fund-java/19-spring-data-jpa-entities-an.explanation:
Compare ids only when both are assigned, and keep the hash constant:

```java
@Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
    if (this == o) return true;
    if (!(o instanceof Order other)) return false;
    Long mine = getId(), theirs = other.getId();
    return mine != null && mine.equals(theirs);
}
@Override public int hashCode() { return Order.class.hashCode(); }
```

The tests catch the IDE-generated version, `Objects.equals(id, other.id)` with `Objects.hash(id)`, used instead of these rules. The hash moves when the id is assigned, so "an entity added to a set before persist is still found after its id is assigned" fails, and two unsaved entities compare equal because null equals null, so "two new entities are different, even with equal fields (boundary)" fails. A version with `getClass() != o.getClass()` or a direct `other.id` field read fails "a lazy proxy equals its entity, both ways, and is found in a set holding it". A constant hash puts every order in one bucket, which is fine for the small sets entities live in; with a stable natural key (an order number) you can hash that instead.

