Persist manages the argument; merge copies onto the managed instance and returns it:

```java
if (entity.id == null) {
    entity.id = nextId++;
    managed.put(entity.id, entity);
    return entity;
}
var target = find(entity.id);           // identity map first, else one select
if (target != entity) target.status = entity.status;
return target;
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong version that puts the detached argument into the persistence context and returns it on merge, instead of copying onto the managed instance. "saving a detached entity returns a different, managed instance with its state" fails because the result is the argument itself, "after a merge, edits to the argument are lost and edits to the result are saved" fails because the argument's later edit is flushed, and "merging onto an instance that is already managed needs no select (boundary)" fails because a second object now stands for the same id. Copying onto the managed instance but returning the argument fails those same three tests, starting with "saving a detached entity returns a different, managed instance with its state". That is the opposite of JPA's contract, and the reason `save`'s return value must be used. Skipping the `managed` lookup in `find` fails "find returns one instance per id with one select".
